    "prompt": "Assume data is generated by $y(t) = \\varphi^\\top(t)\\theta_o + e(t)$, where $e(t)$ is zero-mean stationary noise with variance $\\lambda$ and $\\varphi(t)$ is a stationary stochastic regressor vector. Using the Law of Large Numbers as $N \\to \\infty$, state: (1) the asymptotic limit of $\\hat{\\theta} - \\theta_o$, (2) the two conditions required for $\\hat{\\theta}$ to be consistent ($\\hat{\\theta} \\to \\theta_o$), and (3) why the ARX least squares estimator is consistent when $e(t)$ is white noise independent of $u(t)$ but typically inconsistent when $e(t)$ is colored noise.",
    "hint": "Write $\\hat{\\theta} - \\theta_o = \\left(\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{t=1}^N \\varphi(t)\\varphi^\\top(t)\\right)^{-1} \\left(\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{t=1}^N \\varphi(t)e(t)\\right)$ and let $N \\to \\infty$. See `LinReg2021.pdf` pp. 14-15 and `L13.pdf` pp. 28-29.",
    "options": null,
    "correctAnswer": "1. Asymptotic limit (`LinReg2021.pdf` p. 14, `L13.pdf` p. 29):\nBy the Law of Large Numbers, as $N \\to \\infty$:\n$$\\hat{\\theta} - \\theta_o = \\left(\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{t=1}^N \\varphi(t)\\varphi^\\top(t)\\right)^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{t=1}^N \\varphi(t)e(t)\\right) \\to \\left(\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)\\varphi^\\top(t)]\\right)^{-1} \\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)e(t)].$$\n\n2. Two Conditions for Consistency ($\\hat{\\theta} \\to \\theta_o$):\n- Condition 1 (Identifiability / Excitation): The matrix $\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)\\varphi^\\top(t)]$ must be invertible (satisfied if the input $u(t)$ is persistently exciting, i.e., contains enough frequencies).\n- Condition 2 (Uncorrelatedness): $\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)e(t)] = 0$, i.e., the regressor $\\varphi(t)$ and the noise term $e(t)$ at time $t$ must be uncorrelated.\n\n3. ARX with White vs Colored Noise (`LinReg2021.pdf` p. 15):\nIn an ARX model, $\\varphi(t) = [-y(t-1) \\; \\dots \\; -y(t-n_a) \\; u(t-1) \\; \\dots \\; u(t-n_b)]^\\top$ contains past outputs $y(t-k)$ ($k \\ge 1$). If $e(t)$ is white noise independent of $u(t)$, then $y(t-k)$ depends only on $e(s)$ for $s \\le t-k < t$, which is uncorrelated with $e(t)$, so $\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)e(t)] = 0$ and $\\hat{\\theta}$ is consistent (with asymptotic covariance $\\text{Cov}(\\hat{\\theta}) \\approx \\frac{\\lambda}{N}\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)\\varphi^\\top(t)]^{-1}$). If $e(t)$ is colored noise, $e(t)$ is correlated with $e(t-1)$, which is part of $y(t-1)$ inside $\\varphi(t)$, so $\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(t)e(t)] \\ne 0$ and the ARX LS estimate has an asymptotic bias (inconsistent).",
