    "roundingNote": null,
    "correctedStatement": "In the linear model structure $y(t) = \\frac{b_1 q^{-1}}{a_0 + a_1 q^{-1}} u(t) + e(t)$, multiplying $a_0, a_1, b_1$ by any non-zero constant $c$ yields the exact same transfer function, so we must fix $a_0 = 1$ (monic denominator) to achieve a unique parametrization regardless of the input signal.",
    "distractorExplanations": null,
    "explanation": "Identifiability requires two distinct properties: (1) a unique **parametrization** (different parameter vectors $\\theta$ must yield different transfer functions) and (2) **informative data** (such as a persistently exciting input $u(t)$).\n\nHere, for any non-zero scalar $c \\neq 0$,\n$$\\frac{(c b_1) q^{-1}}{(c a_0) + (c a_1) q^{-1}} = \\frac{b_1 q^{-1}}{a_0 + a_1 q^{-1}}$$\nThus, infinitely many triplets $(c a_0, c a_1, c b_1)$ produce the exact same input-output relation. Even if $u(t)$ is white noise, the parameters cannot be uniquely determined unless we normalize the denominator polynomial by fixing $a_0 = 1$."
