    "tolerance": null,
    "unit": null,
    "roundingNote": null,
    "correctedStatement": null,
    "distractorExplanations": null,
    "explanation": "This addresses a classic exam misconception! Writing the model in matrix form $Y = \\Phi K + v$ with $\\varphi(t) = u(t-1)$, the estimation error is:\n$$\\hat{K} - K = (\\Phi^\\top \\Phi)^{-1} \\Phi^\\top v$$\nWhen $u(t)$ is deterministic (or independent of $v(t)$ in open loop), we can pull the regressor matrix factor outside the expectation:\n$$\\mathbb{E}[\\hat{K} - K] = (\\Phi^\\top \\Phi)^{-1} \\Phi^\\top \\mathbb{E}[v] = 0$$\nNotice that **only** $\\mathbb{E}[v(t)] = 0$ (zero mean) and invertibility of $\\Phi^\\top \\Phi$ are required for unbiasedness! Whether $v(t)$ is white or coloured affects the **covariance** $\\text{Cov}(\\hat{K}) = (\\Phi^\\top \\Phi)^{-1}\\Phi^\\top R \\Phi (\\Phi^\\top \\Phi)^{-1}$, but does **not** introduce bias in an open-loop FIR model (unlike in an ARX model, where $\\varphi(t)$ contains lagged outputs $y(t-1)$ correlated with coloured noise $v(t)$)."
