    "explanation": "By the law of large numbers, as $N \\to \\infty$:\n$$\\hat{b}_{\\text{IV}} \\to \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[y(t-1)y(t)]}{\\mathbb{E}[y(t-1)\\hat{u}(t-1)]}$$\nSubstitute $y(t-1) = b_o u(t-2) + e(t-1)$, $y(t) = b_o u(t-1) + e(t)$, and $\\hat{u}(t-1) = u(t-1) + v(t-1)$:\n1. Denominator:\n$$\\mathbb{E}[y(t-1)\\hat{u}(t-1)] = \\mathbb{E}[(b_o u(t-2) + e(t-1))(u(t-1) + v(t-1))] = b_o \\mathbb{E}[u(t-2)u(t-1)] = b_o R_u(1)$$\nsince $e(t-1)$ and $v(t-1)$ are uncorrelated with each other and with $u$.\n2. Numerator:\n$$\\mathbb{E}[y(t-1)y(t)] = \\mathbb{E}[(b_o u(t-2) + e(t-1))(b_o u(t-1) + e(t))] = b_o^2 \\mathbb{E}[u(t-2)u(t-1)] = b_o^2 R_u(1)$$\nsince $e(t)$ is white noise uncorrelated with $u$.\n\nBecause $u(t)$ is an AR(1) process with pole $0.5 \\neq 0$, $R_u(1) = 0.5 R_u(0) = \\frac{2}{3}\\lambda_w \\neq 0$. Dividing numerator by denominator gives:\n$$\\hat{b}_{\\text{IV}} \\to \\frac{b_o^2 R_u(1)}{b_o R_u(1)} = b_o$$\nHere $y(t-1)$ acts as an **instrumental variable**: it is correlated with the true regressor $u(t-1)$ ($R_u(1) \\neq 0$) but uncorrelated with both noises $v(t-1)$ and $e(t)$."
