    "explanation": "From Lecture 10 (Slides 19–23), the regularized LS estimator $\\hat{\\theta}^R = (\\Phi^\\top\\Phi + \\gamma P^{-1})^{-1}\\Phi^\\top Y$ achieves minimum theoretical MSE when $\\gamma^\\star = \\lambda$ and $P^\\star = \\theta_o\\theta_o^\\top$. Although $\\theta_o$ is unknown, we know for a stable LTI system that its impulse response $g(\\ell)$ decays exponentially as $\\ell$ grows and is smooth. Standard Ridge regression ($P = I_n$) penalizes all impulse response lags $g(0), \\dots, g(n)$ equally and ignores inter-lag correlation. The Tuned-Correlated (TC) kernel $[P(\\alpha)]_{ij} = c\\alpha^{\\max(i,j)}$ ($0 \\le \\alpha \\le 1$) has diagonal entries $c\\alpha^i$ that decay exponentially with lag $i$ (penalizing later lags more strongly) and positive off-diagonal correlations, encoding stability and smoothness."
