    "unit": null,
    "roundingNote": null,
    "correctedStatement": "For data generated by dynamical systems (time-series data), the sequential time order is essential because $y(t)$ depends on lagged inputs/outputs $u(t-1), y(t-1), \\dots$; therefore, the data must be split into contiguous time blocks (e.g., the first half $t = 1, \\dots, 50$ for estimation and the second half $t = 51, \\dots, 100$ for validation), whereas odd/even splitting is only valid for static problems $y_k = f(x_k) + e_k$.",
    "distractorExplanations": null,
    "explanation": "The statement is **False**. As emphasized on Slide 25 of Lecture 10 (`L10_Regularization_2022-Ver1.pdf`), for static problems $y_k = f(x_k) + e_k$, the order of data points is not important so odd/even splitting is fine. However, for dynamical systems $y(t) = G(q)u(t) + e(t)$, data arrives sequentially in time and forming the regressor $\\varphi(t)$ or simulating the model requires consecutive samples. Splitting into odd and even indices destroys the temporal lag structure; instead, one must split the time series into contiguous segments, such as the first half $t=1,\\dots,50$ for estimation and the second half $t=51,\\dots,100$ for validation (and finally re-estimate on the complete dataset once the hyperparameters are chosen)."
