    "options": null,
    "correctAnswer": "Two main issues arise when identifying an ARX model of the inverted pendulum during upright closed-loop stabilization:\n\n1. **Lack of persistent excitation / uninformative closed-loop data:**\nWhen balancing the pendulum around the upright equilibrium using feedback control (without injecting an external reference/dither signal), the controller's goal is to keep $\\theta(t) \\approx \\pi$ constant. Moreover, if $u(t)$ is generated by a linear feedback law from the measured output, the regressors become collinear. Thus, the collected data may not be informative enough (not persistently exciting) to uniquely or accurately identify the model.\n\n2. **Restrictive ARX noise model ($H(q) = 1/A(q)$) and closed-loop bias:**\nIn closed-loop identification, the noise model must capture both external disturbances and any unmodeled dynamics/linearization errors. Because an ARX model forces the noise transfer function $H(q) = 1/A(q)$ to share poles with the system transfer function $G(q) = B(q)/A(q)$, its noise model is very restrictive and generally cannot capture the true noise properties, causing biased parameter estimates in closed loop.",
